# tests/fixtures/statcast_header.sha256 -- DT-02, the committed header fixture.
#
# SOP W2.9: "Header stability -- 119 columns, byte-identical across 2024, 2025
# and 2026 and identical to the minors CSV; assert the header sha256 against a
# committed fixture on every day, including 2022 and 2023, which were
# unverified before this project."
#
# WHAT IS HASHED, exactly. The first line of the response body as it arrives:
# the three BOM bytes included, the line terminator excluded. Savant serves LF,
# not CRLF; a trailing CR would be stripped before hashing so a line-ending
# change reads as a line-ending change and not as a column change. The digest
# therefore covers the BOM as well, which is deliberate: DT-02 says
# byte-identical, and the bytes start at byte zero.
#
# A second digest is recorded for diagnosis only. It is the same line decoded
# with utf-8-sig and re-encoded utf-8, so the BOM is gone. When the two digests
# disagree in the same direction the BOM moved; when only the BOM-stripped one
# moves, a column moved.
#
# MEASURED by the W2.6 run over the 799 MLB days on disk, seasons 2022 through
# 2026 -- quality/receipts/W2.6.json carries the timestamp. This file is read by
# the scanner as code, and its comments are not stripped, so a run date at or
# past the boundary cannot be written here even as a stamp (GD-04 rule 5):
# one digest, 799 of 799 days, 119 columns, header line 1,825 bytes with BOM.
# absump.ingest.statcast_day reads this file; tests/data/test_statcast_days.py
# asserts it against every day in the raw lake.
#
# Format: "<sha256>  <name>", two spaces, as sha256sum writes it. Comment lines
# and blank lines are ignored.

c1703bd38919ac5304ed7f1e0f3eb225f385b969203db316cde88adc409462e3  statcast_csv_header
15abe7168e2a5e293574daff1c87b288364e8dcea508a4c52431d9e8fbab269c  statcast_csv_header_nobom
